# Vercel Speed Insights Configuration

Successfully configured Vercel Speed Insights for this Next.js e-commerce project.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
- `app/layout.tsx` - Added SpeedInsights component to root layout

## Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Speed Insights documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart), I implemented the following:

1. **Package Installation**: The `@vercel/speed-insights` package (v2.0.0) was already installed in the project dependencies.

2. **SpeedInsights Component Configuration**: Added the SpeedInsights component to the root layout file following Next.js App Router best practices:
   - Imported `SpeedInsights` from `@vercel/speed-insights/next`
   - Added `<SpeedInsights />` component inside the `<body>` tag alongside the existing `<Analytics />` component

3. **Framework-Specific Implementation**: This project uses Next.js 16.3.5 with the App Router architecture, so I used the Next.js-specific import path (`@vercel/speed-insights/next`) as recommended in the documentation.

## Testing & Verification

✅ Build verification: `npm run build` completed successfully with no errors
✅ Linting: `npm run lint` passed (pre-existing warnings in other files unrelated to this change)
✅ TypeScript compilation: No type errors
✅ No test suite available in the project

## How Speed Insights Works

Once deployed to Vercel, Speed Insights will:
- Automatically collect Core Web Vitals metrics (LCP, FID, CLS, etc.)
- Track real user performance data
- Display analytics in the Vercel dashboard under the Speed Insights tab
- Help identify performance bottlenecks and optimization opportunities

## Next Steps

After deployment to Vercel, you can:
1. Visit your Vercel dashboard
2. Navigate to your project
3. Click on "Speed Insights" in the sidebar
4. View real-time performance metrics from actual users

The SpeedInsights component will only collect data in production environments and has minimal impact on page performance.
